transcript.text:
What you describe is usually normal for a V15 Detect in Auto/Med mode, especially with the Digital Motorbar/High Torque-style floor head.

When the head moves from hard tile onto a rug/carpet, the machine detects the increased brush/head resistance and can automatically increase motor power. That causes:

• a noticeably louder motor sound;
• greater battery use while on the rug;
• the remaining run-time figure on the LCD to reduce faster, because it is an estimate based on the current power draw;
• the display/bar graph to update as the load and detected dust level change.

When you return to tiles, it needs less power, so it quietens and the run-time estimate may rise or fall less quickly. That behaviour alone does not suggest the motor is failing.

A motor or head fault would be more likely if it is doing any of these:

• repeatedly surging/pulsing on the same surface;
• stopping and starting, or displaying a blockage/error message;
• making a grinding, screeching, burning, or rattling noise;
• losing suction badly;
• becoming unusually hot;
• draining the battery abnormally even on a hard floor in Eco mode;
• the brush bar fails to turn or is intermittently cutting out.

For reassurance, try this:

1. Check the cleaner head brush bar for wrapped hair, thread, or debris.
2. Check the airway, wand and bin inlet for a partial blockage.
3. Ensure the filter is clean and fully dry before use — Dyson advises at least 24 hours air-drying after washing.
4. Test it briefly in Eco and then Boost on both tile and rug. A change in noise and run time between surfaces is most expected in Auto.
5. If you have another head, try that too. This can distinguish a head/brush issue from a machine issue.

You do not need to wait for it to “die completely” if it starts showing the fault signs above. Record a short video showing the mode selected, the floor change, the screen and the sound, then contact Dyson under the guarantee. But based on the specific tile-to-rug behaviour you’ve described, it sounds like the V15’s automatic load adjustment working as designed.
